Day 266/300: Integer to Roman

Problem Statement #12

Problem Statement Explantion:

Roman numerals are represented by seven different symbols: I, V, X, L, C, D and M.Symbol,Value
I,1
V,5
X,10
L,50
C,100
D,500
M,1000

Given an integer, convert it to a Roman numeral. The input is guaranteed to be within the range from 1 to 3999.

Solution Approach:

The core of Roman numeral construction is a greedy algorithm. We always want to use the largest possible symbol to represent the remaining value.

Key Strategy:
Define Special Cases: Standard Roman numerals subtract values in specific cases (e.g., 4 is IV, not IIII). We treat these "subtractive" pairs as their own unique symbols (900, 400, 90, 40, 9, 4).
Mapping: Create two parallel arrays (or a LinkedHashMap) storing the integer values and their corresponding Roman symbols in descending order.
Iterate & Subtract: * Compare the input num with the largest value in our array.
If num is greater than or equal to the value, append the symbol to our result and subtract the value from num.
Repeat until num becomes zero.

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public String intToRoman(int num) {
        int[] values = {1000, 900, 500, 400, 100, 90, 50, 40, 10, 9, 5, 4, 1};
        String[] symbols = {"M", "CM", "D", "CD", "C", "XC", "L", "XL", "X", "IX", "V", "IV", "I"};
        
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length && num > 0; i++) {
            while (num >= values[i]) {
                num -= values[i];
                sb.append(symbols[i]);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(1)
Space Complexity: O(1)








